  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "c5d04bba-638c-4b67-9615-70298e309439",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Notes/Summary\n",
    "\n",
    "Taking a daily capacity of 1.2 million appointments and taking a 30 day month it, at first glance appears that utilisation is nowhere near capacity. The lineplot shows this clearly. Indeed the highest calculated utilisation figure is 85.4% (November 2021), with the lowest being 44.5% April 2020 - just after the start of the first covid lockdown.\n",
    "\n",
    "But capacity is assumed to be the same thoughout the whole data period, including weekends and covid lockdowns. This is distoring the data and giving a false impression of actual utilisation\n",
    "\n",
    "Plotting the number of appointments by HCP types, shows that GP appointments are the most common, although other practice staff aren't far beind. What these are isn't defined but would assumed to be things like, practice nurse, mental health services etc\n",
    "\n",
    "Appointment status (whether an appointment was actually attended or not) by month shows that the vast majority of appointments are in fact attended. Interestingly it shows a significant drop in attended appointments when lockdown begins. Were these appointments not rescheduled by the GP pracice?\n",
    "\n",
    "Removing 'Attended' from the appointment status and replotting the graph gives a much clearer picture of what is going om. During the first covid lockdown, the number of DNA's fell dramatically, but at the sglglyame time so did the numnber of actual appointments. Interestingly the number of 'unknown' appointment statuses increased dramatically. Were appoinmnents not being recorded efficiently during this period? The number sharply drops in Aril 2020.\n",
    "\n",
    "Turning to time between booking an appointment and actually attended it can be seem that same day appointments are the most common. However, these only account for approxiamtely 50% of all appoitments, but it should be remembered these are not just GP appointments. The second highest waiting time was 2 - 7 days.\n",
    "\n",
    "At the first covid lockdown (23rd March 2020) all 'time between booking and attending appointments' dropped, as a result of reduced appoinment availability and cancellations.\n",
    "\n",
    "The boxplot of appointment distribution by service setting shows GP appointments shows the interquartile range of appointments (Those values inside the box).\n",
    "\n",
